WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.029
- Bondi Beach is one of
the most popular beaches

00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:03.120
in Australia.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.008
Bondi Rescue is a reality TV show

00:00:05.008 --> 00:00:07.960
that covers some of the drama
that goes down on the beach.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.630
Here are some of the most
wild medical emergencies

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:12.220
that have happened on the show.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.326
Beewoop!

00:00:13.326 --> 00:00:14.587
- Get him outta the water, ready?

00:00:14.587 --> 00:00:15.420
And then we'll start and
compressions up here.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.170
- All right, super important here,

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.450
getting people obviously
out of the water for safety,

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.700
everyone's safety is of utmost priority.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.330
Calling for help is the
number one thing you do.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:24.163
You don't just yell it,

00:00:24.163 --> 00:00:25.980
you point to someone
and say, you call 9-1-1.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.510
Otherwise, everyone assumes
everyone else calls 9-1-1.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:31.040
And, I know I always say start
chest compressions first,

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.490
but for drowning victims,

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.290
you do two rescue breaths
first, mouth to mouth.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:38.610
'Cause the mechanism of why
someone's heart has stopped

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.260
and they're not breathing in the water

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.500
is very different than
what happens on land.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.069
- Okay, okay, who's doing,

00:00:44.069 --> 00:00:45.440
I'm going, I'm going, I'm going.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.990
- One, two, three, four, five, six,

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:49.394
seven, eight, nine, 10.

00:00:49.394 --> 00:00:50.594
(inaudible cross talk)

00:00:50.594 --> 00:00:52.710
- Good depth.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.600
Two inches deep on those
chest compressions.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.652
Good speed, 100 beats per minute.

00:00:56.652 --> 00:00:59.640
- 19, 20. One, two, three, four, five.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.700
- All right, so the
fact that he's vomiting

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.150
means, right away, you
have to turn him over

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.048
onto his side in order to
help clear that airway.

00:01:05.048 --> 00:01:06.450
In drowning emergencies,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.240
it's truly airway, breathing, circulation.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:09.580
Like, you have to focus on the breathing.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:11.640
When you find someone on
the street who's collapsed

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.020
and heart has stopped,
you can do hands only CPR.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:15.570
But here, you have to
think about the airway.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:16.730
Let me explain the
difference here, actually.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:17.820
This is important nuance.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.820
When you find someone on the
street and they've collapsed,

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.180
their heart has stopped beating.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.630
But, they've recently just
oxygenated their blood,

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:26.290
so there's still some residual
oxygen left in the blood

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.280
throughout the body, even though
it's delivered most of it.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.483
So, as you perform hands only CPR,

00:01:29.483 --> 00:01:31.520
you can circulate that blood

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.320
and deliver that remaining oxygen.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.630
When someone has drowned,

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.650
they usually have no oxygen reserves,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.650
therefore are very hypoxic in their blood.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.960
Doing hands only CPR
does them a disservice,

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.490
instead of giving them
some rescue breaths.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:43.780
Also, a lot of people think that

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.710
the lungs get filled up with water.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:47.330
The airway actually clamps itself off

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.330
and prevents water from
going to the lungs,

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.244
and whatever small amount of
water gets into the lungs,

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.280
actually gets absorbed systemically.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.310
So, it's really about
getting oxygen in there

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.170
as quickly as possible and
pumping to help the heart

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.050
pump that blood throughout the body,

00:02:00.050 --> 00:02:02.010
by actually squeezing
the heart, physically.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.277
- Can I get someone to
come up and get a defib?

00:02:03.277 --> 00:02:04.800
- [Narrator] The defib is in the tower,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.370
but Dono can't leave the
control post unmanned.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.364
- The defibrillator is
something that we use

00:02:09.364 --> 00:02:12.396
to check if the patient has
any sort of shockable rhythm.

00:02:12.396 --> 00:02:14.100
A lot of times in Gray's Anatomy,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.560
you'll see someone go flat line.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.520
They say, oh my God, get the
defibrillator, shock them.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.880
That's not a shockable rhythm.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:19.910
What happens is,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:21.948
the defibrillator actually
stops the rhythm of your heart,

00:02:21.948 --> 00:02:25.610
breaks that rhythm, and
allows it to restart.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.410
So, if someone doesn't have a rhythm,

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.320
they're completely flat,
and you reshock them,

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.180
you make it flat again, that
doesn't help them at all.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.250
At that point, it's more about
doing the chest compressions

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.340
and giving them medications

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.001
to advance cardiac life
support, like epinephrine.

00:02:36.001 --> 00:02:39.150
- [Narrator] He's now been clinically dead

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.470
for three minutes.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:41.320
- 21, 22, 23, 24-

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:44.124
(inaudible cross talking)

00:02:44.124 --> 00:02:45.277
- And the way you're
doing this is 30 pumps

00:02:45.277 --> 00:02:47.030
for every two breaths.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:49.143
So you do 30 pumps at a
speed of 100 per minute.

00:02:49.143 --> 00:02:50.954
Give two breaths, continue.

00:02:50.954 --> 00:02:52.760
- [Narrator] It's critical that no one

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.658
touches the man's body
while it is shocked.

00:02:54.658 --> 00:02:55.491
- He's my friend.

00:02:55.491 --> 00:02:56.906
- I know you can't touch him.

00:02:56.906 --> 00:02:57.900
- [Responder 1] Don't touch him.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:58.733
- [Responder 2] Don't touch him.

00:02:58.733 --> 00:02:59.912
- [Responder 3] Don't touch him.

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:00.745
- [Responder 1] You'll get electrocuted.

00:03:00.745 --> 00:03:02.367
- [Defibrillator] Check
airway, check breathing.

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:04.810
If needed, begin CPR.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:06.710
- So the modern
defibrillators are really good

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:08.090
in that they analyze the rhythms

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.560
and you don't have to look at the rhythm

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:10.920
and try and figure out what's going on.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.870
It will say non-shockable
rhythm, continue CPR.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:16.870
Or shockable rhythm, remove
all people from defibrillator.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:17.703
Then you hit shock.

00:03:17.703 --> 00:03:19.820
It says shocking on three.
Shock, shock, shock.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.000
And then it'll deliver the shock

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.830
so that you know not to
be touching the person.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.188
- [Responder 1] I've got
a pulse. I have a pulse.

00:03:24.188 --> 00:03:25.954
- [Responder 2] He's back,
he's back. He's responding.

00:03:25.954 --> 00:03:27.665
- [Responder 1] Let's go. Let's go.

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:30.451
See if he wants to vomit.

00:03:30.451 --> 00:03:32.380
See, he's got a lot of
fluid coming out, huh?

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.600
- [Responder 2] That's
what we want. That's good.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:35.788
- Don't keep bagging him.

00:03:35.788 --> 00:03:37.441
If he's conscious and he's breathing.

00:03:37.441 --> 00:03:39.960
I know there's a lot of
stuff happening right now,

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.040
but you could actually
force some of that vomiting

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:43.610
aspiration back into the lungs.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:44.914
You don't want to do that.

00:03:44.914 --> 00:03:45.880
(man retching)

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:47.466
Put him on his side.

00:03:47.466 --> 00:03:48.679
No, take the ambu bag off.

00:03:48.679 --> 00:03:50.300
(clapping)

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:51.680
He's still not out of danger yet.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.560
Everyone stop clapping
like you just watched

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:54.796
a Jerry Springer episode.

00:03:54.796 --> 00:03:56.600
(clapping)

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.177
He was without oxygen for
a long period of time.

00:03:58.177 --> 00:04:00.450
And that guy keeps putting
this stupid ambu mask on him.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.720
Stop it. He's awake now.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.000
- [Narrator] Portuguese
Man O' War Jellyfish,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.140
commonly known as blue bottles,

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.930
are blown onto the coast by onshore winds.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.236
Blue bottles have rightly earned
the label, floating terror.

00:04:12.236 --> 00:04:17.020
- I've seen thousands of
people that have been stung

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:19.689
by blue bottles, but this day,

00:04:19.689 --> 00:04:21.890
it was the biggest welt I've ever seen.

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:22.723
- Oh.

00:04:22.723 --> 00:04:24.720
Yeah, they basically leave
like whiplash looking marks

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.720
on your skin, creating
a type of dermatitis.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.280
It's actually really
cool how this happens.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.100
So they have these nematocysts

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.810
that are at the end of their test-

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.018
Testicles.

00:04:33.018 --> 00:04:34.016
(laughing)

00:04:34.016 --> 00:04:35.032
Tentacles.

00:04:35.032 --> 00:04:35.910
When they hit you with
them and they touch you,

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.360
they actually attach to your skin,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:38.610
and then inject the poison in.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.110
And there's been some evidence

00:04:40.956 --> 00:04:41.880
that putting vinegar onto them

00:04:42.991 --> 00:04:43.824
can actually help deactivate that venom

00:04:43.824 --> 00:04:45.310
and those nematocysts.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:46.740
- [Narrator] Nine year old Zach

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.864
has suffered a blue bottle
sting over his entire leg.

00:04:48.864 --> 00:04:51.919
(Zach screaming)

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:53.574
- Zach, that's enough, enough.

00:04:53.574 --> 00:04:55.854
(Zach screaming)

00:04:55.854 --> 00:04:58.090
- All right, dad, maybe
give your son a hug.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.070
Like there's toughening your kid up,

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.423
but when they're experiencing
real big pain, give him a hug.

00:05:02.423 --> 00:05:05.186
- [Narrator] Zach's father
is back on his feet.

00:05:05.186 --> 00:05:08.040
As lifeguards administer
the green whistle.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.160
- What you gotta do is, you
gotta put it in your mouth

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.350
like this, all right?

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:13.370
And just breathe it in.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:15.197
And it's gonna take the pain away for you.

00:05:15.197 --> 00:05:17.449
- The kid's like, give me it!

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:18.594
- There you go.

00:05:18.594 --> 00:05:20.014
- Oh, wow

00:05:20.014 --> 00:05:21.493
- Good man.

00:05:21.493 --> 00:05:22.824
- Huh? The sweet sound of silence.

00:05:22.824 --> 00:05:24.900
(Mike laughing)

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.430
- These lifeguards are like,
"can you shut this kid up?"

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:29.663
They got the kid high and he calmed down.

00:05:29.663 --> 00:05:31.180
(static crinkling)

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:32.920
- I'm really allergic to peanuts.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:35.187
- Oh no. I hope she's not
having an anaphylactic attack.

00:05:35.187 --> 00:05:37.336
- [Lifeguard] And have you had
an allergic reaction before?

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:38.412
- Yeah.

00:05:38.412 --> 00:05:39.541
- [Lifeguard] Is this what you felt last?

00:05:39.541 --> 00:05:40.599
- I know this feeling. Yeah.

00:05:40.599 --> 00:05:42.387
- [Narrator] Left
untreated, Victoria's throat

00:05:42.387 --> 00:05:45.150
will become so swollen, she
may be unable to breathe.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:47.740
- This type of allergic
reaction, called anaphylaxis,

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:50.480
actually leads to
swelling of the air pipe.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.820
It also can create swelling of the mouth.

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:55.030
It can lead to nausea,
vomiting in some cases.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.110
And one of the most dangerous
things about anaphylaxis,

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:58.340
outside of closing your throat,

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:00.300
is the major drop of blood pressure.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.190
'Cause it dilates your blood vessels,

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.200
therefore tanking your blood pressure,

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:06.395
not getting enough blood
circulation to those vital organs.

00:06:06.395 --> 00:06:08.200
It can potentially lead to death.

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.080
This is why a lot of people think,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.580
oh just treat it with Benadryl.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.580
But Benadryl doesn't treat
that part of anaphylaxis.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.800
That's why we need to use these EpiPens

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.945
to boost up the cardiac output

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:21.760
and decrease the swelling
of the oral pathway.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.717
- [Narrator] Lifeguard's best
chance of saving Victoria

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.950
is via a shot of
adrenaline from an EpiPen.

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:29.700
- And I'm sure many of
you have heard the story.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:31.740
I've had to do something
like this in the air,

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.720
while flying on a gentleman
who did not have an EpiPen.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.440
So we had to concoct the
EpiPen through medications

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:38.530
that you would actually use
if someone's heart stops

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.880
and they were flat lining.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:42.570
Same medication, different dosage.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.880
I had to do that calculation
kind of on the fly.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:46.920
There was no wifi 'cause we were

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:47.753
flying over the Atlantic Ocean.

00:06:47.753 --> 00:06:48.586
It was a crazy scene.

00:06:48.586 --> 00:06:51.200
And also, this auto injector is nice

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.610
and thin and small the needle.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:54.320
So it's not gonna cause
a lot of discomfort.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.490
The one that I had to use
is made for a crash cart.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.170
So it's like a really thick, long needle.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:06:59.750
And I had to jam it into his thigh.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:00.930
He was not happy with me,

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:03.464
but he was happy after
the fact when he survived.

00:07:03.464 --> 00:07:05.542
- Thank you Dr. Mike, I really
appreciate your help, man.

00:07:05.542 --> 00:07:06.573
- So I hit her with the EpiPen, bang.

00:07:06.573 --> 00:07:08.190
Held it in for 10 seconds,

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.240
and I thought that was gonna be it.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:11.508
Okay lay down sweetheart.

00:07:11.508 --> 00:07:12.736
There we go.

00:07:12.736 --> 00:07:15.090
You all right?

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:16.800
Stay with us. You're all right?

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.660
When she dropped on us, I was so scared.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.640
And I thought maybe this would be a resus.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.420
- I suspect what's happening here

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.460
is probably a vasovagal reaction.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:26.930
When you get really nervous
that she essentially

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:29.097
lost blood supply to
her brain temporarily,

00:07:29.097 --> 00:07:31.150
from seeing the needle go into her thigh.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.400
This happens quite often.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.240
In fact, the gentleman
after I injected him

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.790
with the syringe, he started telling me

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:38.780
that he thought his
throat was feeling weird

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:39.613
and closing again.

00:07:39.613 --> 00:07:41.890
But when I looked into it, it
actually was completely open.

00:07:41.890 --> 00:07:43.620
I had to use my iPhone
flashlight to do so.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.180
I realized what was happening was because

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:46.900
I gave him adrenaline,
his throat dried up.

00:07:46.900 --> 00:07:48.210
His mouth dried up like you would

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.070
when you're giving a
speech and you get nervous.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:51.880
And as a result, he felt
like it was closing again,

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:52.790
but it actually wasn't.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.770
So it was good that I
was able to check that

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:56.820
and not necessarily stick him again

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:58.940
with an unnecessary medication and needle.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:00.720
- [Narrator] Andrea and her cousin Gerald

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:03.486
were taking advantage of
iconic Bondi as a backdrop

00:08:03.486 --> 00:08:05.690
for a rollerblading video,

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:08.047
when things went horribly wrong.

00:08:08.047 --> 00:08:08.880
- Whoa.

00:08:09.802 --> 00:08:13.020
- Oh! Patella dislocation.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.050
It's actually really easy
to pop back in, but wow.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:16.590
Really all you do is you put pressure

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:19.080
on the outside of the leg and
extend a leg at the same time,

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.540
and then it just puts it
right back into place.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.081
- And I'm gonna just gently
manipulate your knee.

00:08:23.081 --> 00:08:25.320
- I mean, I like your wording.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:26.870
I know it's not gonna be gentle.

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:28.599
(Mike laughing)

00:08:28.599 --> 00:08:29.432
- Keep breathing.

00:08:29.432 --> 00:08:30.265
- There you go.

00:08:30.265 --> 00:08:32.130
Extend and pressure,
pressure, pressure, pressure.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:33.170
A little more pressure.

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:35.113
- And he rolled up.

00:08:35.113 --> 00:08:35.946
- There you go.

00:08:35.946 --> 00:08:36.779
And it's gonna pop right back in.

00:08:36.779 --> 00:08:37.612
There you go.

00:08:37.612 --> 00:08:39.883
It goes from pain level 10
to zero almost instantly.

00:08:39.883 --> 00:08:43.270
- [Narrator] A man's found
floating lifeless in the surf.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:45.110
- See that's sometimes
the worst situation.

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:47.370
'Cause you don't know how long
they've been under that surf.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:48.720
- [Narrator] Hoppo can't detect a pulse.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.070
- Then right away, two rescue
breaths, chest compressions.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:51.903
Let's go.

00:08:51.903 --> 00:08:53.410
- [Narrator] He's not breathing.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:54.894
- [Hoppo] Baggie. Come on, come on.

00:08:54.894 --> 00:08:56.099
Bagging.

00:08:56.099 --> 00:08:57.390
- [Narrator] Tucker is clinically dead.

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.831
The machine reads Tucker's vital signs.

00:08:59.831 --> 00:09:02.873
- [Defibrillator] Do not touch
patient. Analyzing rhythm.

00:09:02.873 --> 00:09:05.527
- I can't get one. Start CPR.

00:09:05.527 --> 00:09:08.270
- [Narrator] The defib doesn't
work on Tucker first time.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:09.220
- Oh, much faster.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.600
He needs to go much faster
with those chest compressions.

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:13.820
- [Narrator] The third shock
has finally had an effect.

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:15.177
- It's getting stronger.

00:09:15.177 --> 00:09:16.224
It's a good pulse.

00:09:16.224 --> 00:09:17.057
Good pulse here.

00:09:17.057 --> 00:09:19.091
- When you get someone
back from having no pulse

00:09:19.091 --> 00:09:21.260
or no breathing, it's an amazing feeling.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.410
The adrenaline rush of it.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:23.570
- Yeah. I can attest to that.

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:24.640
For them they're doing this

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.380
usually on younger, healthy people.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:28.654
I'm doing this sometimes
on people who are older,

00:09:28.654 --> 00:09:30.660
who have a lot of medical conditions.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.230
They have a young healthy person

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:34.250
who got really sick really
fast cause they drowned.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:35.083
Then they rescued them,

00:09:35.083 --> 00:09:36.560
they restart their heart
and now they're better.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:38.150
And they're almost like
back to normal real quick.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:41.250
For me I have to then now
stabilize that patient,

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.270
get medications going, check their vitals,

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.150
make sure that if their
blood pressure's too low,

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.970
get them on pressers to artificially

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:47.920
raise their blood pressure,

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.950
get them admitted into the ICU,

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:51.820
transfer them to a different team of care.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:53.050
But this is tough because

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:54.590
you cannot control this environment.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.000
I have much more control of my environment

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.208
when I'm in the hospital.

00:09:57.208 --> 00:09:59.100
- I stood on sea urchin.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:00.644
- Oh I don't know about sea urchins.

00:10:00.644 --> 00:10:04.610
- A sea urchin is a little sea creature

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:06.480
with all these little
spikes that come off it

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.106
and they kind of hide
in the rock crevices.

00:10:09.106 --> 00:10:10.280
- Oh!

00:10:10.280 --> 00:10:13.313
Dude. Why do people go in the ocean?

00:10:13.313 --> 00:10:15.230
Just hang out on shore.

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:16.944
- [Narrator] Sea urchin
spikes, carry toxin

00:10:16.944 --> 00:10:19.750
and are extremely brittle.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.853
Like needles of fine
porcelain, they easily break.

00:10:21.853 --> 00:10:25.056
- And yet we tell kids go play in ocean!

00:10:25.056 --> 00:10:28.360
There's spikes with poison in there.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.360
Future generations will
look at us and be like,

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:31.650
what's wrong with these people?

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:33.010
- [Narrator] Jesse and Harrison

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:34.750
start the delicate operation.

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:35.583
- Oh man.

00:10:35.583 --> 00:10:37.378
- [Narrator] Removing
the spines one at a time.

00:10:37.378 --> 00:10:40.100
- Can they like numb him up?

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.912
Give him a little lidocaine. Jesus Christ.

00:10:41.912 --> 00:10:44.599
- [Harrison] Oh, look at that one.

00:10:44.599 --> 00:10:46.190
- Ah! Look at that one!

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:48.233
- Drink. Drink.

00:10:48.233 --> 00:10:51.646
- I was told that he was
having low sugar levels.

00:10:51.646 --> 00:10:54.410
I've dealt with diabetics and
low blood sugar levels before.

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:57.080
And I know that they need soft drink

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:58.070
or they need something sweet

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.500
to bring those sugar levels back up.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:01.521
- He could be hypoglycemic
as they're saying,

00:11:01.521 --> 00:11:03.299
cause those who are diabetic

00:11:03.299 --> 00:11:05.550
struggle to control their blood sugars.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.664
And sometimes they do drop,

00:11:07.664 --> 00:11:09.375
especially if they give themself

00:11:09.375 --> 00:11:10.860
excess medication by accident.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.440
- And then he pulled
out from around his neck

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.800
that he also was an epileptic.

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:17.680
- I mean just call an ambulance.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:19.511
Like I hate that they're
always trying to solve

00:11:19.511 --> 00:11:21.700
and figure out everyone's medical history.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:23.370
Clearly this person's
going through something

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:24.203
and they need help.

00:11:24.203 --> 00:11:25.410
And you're not qualified to deliver

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:26.480
that help at this moment.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.263
- Do you want me to call an
ambulance or anything, mate?

00:11:28.263 --> 00:11:29.508
- Yes. Yes.

00:11:29.508 --> 00:11:31.775
- So if you have an epileptic fit.

00:11:31.775 --> 00:11:34.860
- He's clearly having an epileptic fit.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:35.967
- What was your name again?

00:11:35.967 --> 00:11:38.916
What was your name again, mate?

00:11:38.916 --> 00:11:41.841
He's having an epileptic seizure.

00:11:41.841 --> 00:11:44.490
All I could do was hold his pulse

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:45.980
and I was just basically waiting to see

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:46.950
what would happen next.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.010
- That's actually what you
do during one of those.

00:11:49.010 --> 00:11:50.630
If they last over a certain period of time

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:52.070
we can medicate them in the hospital

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:53.680
but otherwise you just
have to let it happen.

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:55.810
In fact, restraining them
and holding them down,

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:57.339
can actually cause muscle damage.

00:11:57.339 --> 00:12:00.243
- You're all right mate.
You're all right matey.

00:12:00.243 --> 00:12:02.147
- It's okay. You're all
right mate. You're with us.

00:12:02.147 --> 00:12:03.540
- You're all right matey.

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:07.090
- Post-ictal phase, which is
this time after your seizure

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:09.370
is actually a very weird time

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:11.860
for not only the patient,
but also for providers

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.320
because patients oftentimes have amnesia

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:15.720
and don't know what has happened.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.000
They don't know where they are.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.200
They sometimes can be
extremely aggressive.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:21.840
So, it's really important
that there's people there

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.640
to keep them calm, explain what happened

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:25.190
and diffuse the situation.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:27.020
I've had patients throw stuff at me.

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:28.316
- Well I'm a type 1 diabetic.

00:12:28.316 --> 00:12:32.739
and what it does is it, lowers my sugar.

00:12:32.739 --> 00:12:36.339
So I can go into a hypo,
any time, anywhere.

00:12:36.339 --> 00:12:39.573
- A person with type 1 Diabetes is someone

00:12:39.573 --> 00:12:42.607
who has an autoimmune
condition that has resulted

00:12:42.607 --> 00:12:45.860
in the destruction of the
cells that produce insulin.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:47.980
So you do not have enough
insulin on your own

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:50.330
to facilitate the sugar leaving your blood

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:52.980
and going into cells where
it needs to function.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:55.205
So what ends up happening is
those patients need insulin,

00:12:55.205 --> 00:12:57.820
artificial insulin to
be injected into them

00:12:57.820 --> 00:12:59.270
in order for them to function normally.

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:01.650
And sometimes if you don't
gauge how much insulin

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:04.320
you're putting in, in
comparison to how much carbs

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:05.500
and sugars you're consuming,

00:13:05.500 --> 00:13:07.900
you could actually cause
yourself to become hypoglycemic,

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:10.360
have very low blood sugar,
which can lead to seizures.

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:12.540
- [Narrator] With the crowded
surf full of beginners,

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.580
it's only a matter of time
before the first collision.

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:19.040
- [Lifeguard] That's sick. That's gross.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.510
- Oh my God.

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:22.830
- [Narrator] Lifeguards won't attempt

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:24.717
to put a severe dislocation back in place.

00:13:24.717 --> 00:13:28.290
- The only time we do a
reduction in the field,

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:31.650
is if we see neurovascular compromise,

00:13:31.650 --> 00:13:33.940
meaning that there's not
enough blood circulation

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:35.730
going to an area, and as a result,

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:37.060
part of that area is dying.

00:13:37.060 --> 00:13:40.354
Then the risks may warrant
doing that procedure.

00:13:40.354 --> 00:13:42.713
Oh my God, is he gonna just pop it in?

00:13:42.713 --> 00:13:44.880
(gagging)

00:13:45.776 --> 00:13:46.950
What a champ.

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:48.481
I can't even watch this.

00:13:48.481 --> 00:13:50.759
- [Lifeguard] Cheers for him.

00:13:50.759 --> 00:13:53.120
(cheering)

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:54.630
- That's terrible because the hand

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:56.390
has so many vital structures in it.

00:13:56.390 --> 00:13:57.610
Blood vessels, nerves.

00:13:57.610 --> 00:13:59.840
This is why we have
hand surgeon specialist.

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:01.547
It's not enough to send someone
to an orthopedic surgeon.

00:14:01.547 --> 00:14:03.340
Send them to a hand surgeon.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:05.348
My guy's just here, like (grunts).

00:14:05.348 --> 00:14:07.630
- It's still slightly out.

00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:09.577
- Slightly out? It's broken my guy.

00:14:09.577 --> 00:14:11.010
- And I get over there,

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:14.385
and there's a gentleman
sitting against the drain pipe,

00:14:14.385 --> 00:14:16.335
in a puddle of water.

00:14:16.335 --> 00:14:21.335
Quite calm at this stage,
saying he's dislocated his hip.

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:23.610
- A dislocated hip could be

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:26.450
one of the most painful things ever.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.160
- [Lifeguard] He's had a hip replacement.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:29.510
- Oh my God.

00:14:29.510 --> 00:14:31.010
They're not ready for the level of pain

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:32.570
this person's about to experience.

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:34.100
- [Narrator] Moving him to the ambulance

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:36.200
will cause enormous pain.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:38.170
Paramedics administer morphine.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:39.003
- Yeah.

00:14:39.003 --> 00:14:40.705
- [Narrator] It's a bumpy
ride over rocks and sand

00:14:40.705 --> 00:14:42.980
before Danny reaches the ambulance.

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:44.767
(screaming)

00:14:44.767 --> 00:14:46.231
- Yeah. He's not faking it

00:14:46.231 --> 00:14:47.508
- You all right?

00:14:47.508 --> 00:14:49.730
- [Narrator] Beau and Harries
help a disoriented man

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:51.966
with a mystery ailment.

00:14:51.966 --> 00:14:53.058
- I don't know.

00:14:53.058 --> 00:14:54.065
I'm going to faint.

00:14:54.065 --> 00:14:55.066
- [Beau] Yeah?

00:14:55.066 --> 00:14:56.123
- [Narrator] His condition is
getting worse by the second.

00:14:56.123 --> 00:14:57.476
- You okay there mate?

00:14:57.476 --> 00:14:59.104
- [Narrator] Suddenly he stops breathing.

00:14:59.104 --> 00:15:02.960
- If he stops breathing, you
start chest compressions!

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.670
He's not a drowning victim.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.324
- [Narrator] Eventually he comes around.

00:15:06.324 --> 00:15:08.710
- That's it relax as much as possible.

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:10.500
- [Narrator] Lifeguards
need his medical history.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.360
- Well, they don't need
his medical history.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:14.130
They need to call an ambulance

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:16.272
and they need his medical history.

00:15:16.272 --> 00:15:18.112
- I don't know. When it
gets cold in the water,

00:15:18.112 --> 00:15:19.560
it makes my skin go itchy and red.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.560
- Oh, he has cold water urticaria.

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:23.970
- [Narrator] Later doctors
confirmed Matt suffers

00:15:23.970 --> 00:15:27.469
a rare allergic reaction to
cold water that causes hives.

00:15:27.469 --> 00:15:31.590
Matt's swim had sent him
into anaphylactic shock.

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:33.360
- Well, an EpiPen would've
gone a long way there

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:34.350
but they couldn't have known that.

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:36.316
You don't recklessly just
give EpiPens to random people.

00:15:36.316 --> 00:15:39.170
Here are some of the
worst fitness mistakes

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:40.070
my patients make.

00:15:40.070 --> 00:15:41.230
Click here to check that out.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:43.310
As always stay happy and healthy.

00:15:43.310 --> 00:15:44.143
Look at my tail.

00:15:45.348 --> 00:15:47.931
(upbeat music)

